A||N||13||Predator||Fog from AC vents||||5||
Z||000000||Predator||08-11-2000||10:33 PM||||Hey guys, <BR>I've been reading posts for the last couple of weeks here and I have really enjoyed it. I have a 85 GT with 18,000 miles on it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. It is completely stock and all original.<BR>It is great and I love driving it. I have one strange thing happening though. When I have the AC on and in Georgia it is on all the time in the summer it produces's a fog after the engine warms up. It doesn't do it all the time, but it does do it most of the time. I thought it might be in the heater core, but if I switch it to heat it stops? I have never seen anything like it before. It isn't just a little fog either, but a lot. Any suggestions would be appreciated. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||EdsB52||08-11-2000||10:44 PM||||Move out of Georgia?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||08-11-2000||10:54 PM||||Does it cloud up the windows, or does it just blow white "smoke" from the vents? If it's just white smoke, I think it's just the air condensing. I had an A/C that did that once, it was kinda bizarre, but I figured that was what it was. If it actually causes the windows to fog, then you need to sign the title over to me right away, it's not safe to own.....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||08-11-2000||11:22 PM||||My other car does that a lot during high humidity days like we are having now. It is just the condensation from the cold dry air meeting humid warm air inside the car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||08-13-2000||11:05 AM||||its only heater core if it leaves a slimey, hard to remove film on the inside of the glass. If not, I wouldnt worry.||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||08-13-2000||12:28 PM||||If you drive far enough and fast enough with your fan on anything other high speed and your temperature all the way down you can actually cause your evaporator(the cooling part) to ice up and not move any air at all.When you get that mist it is raining in your car because the air is so cold the moisture is condensing out of it.It should only happen on high humidity days.I don't know what size(cubic inch displacement)these compressors are,but eight,nine,and ten inch compressors are used to refrigerate truck bodies as big as 18 feet in length.So that can give you some idea as to how much capacity these compressors have to cool the air.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-13-2000||01:00 PM||||The A6 old compressor was typically used in all GM product with cabin air including very large Cadilac and van interiors. That should give you an idea of the capacity. <P>The newer V5 system has varible displacement and will ajust it's output to better suit the car.<P>If the fog doesn't smell of coolant you likely don't have a problem other than blowing condensates. That's annoying but there may not be much you can do about it. Fiero's ventilator will always put in some outside air no mater how the controls are set. this is done intentionally to prevent buildup of CO2 and other, posibly toxic, gasses in the car.<P>Did you try simply not running the AC on max cool or moving the temperature slide toward warm? Moving a little toward warm will get the incoming air above Dew Point which should kill the fog but leave you cool.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||hugh||08-13-2000||01:29 PM||||theogre How in the world do they vary the displacement of the compressor.I've been in the refrigeration business for over 30 years and have only seen that done on big compressors by unloading cylinders.||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||08-14-2000||02:34 AM||||By varying the angle of a swashplate drive, maybe?||||reg||5
Z||000009||theogre||08-14-2000||03:41 PM||||As far as I know it's what Stu said. I don't have all the info on this compressor. I have service data but not all the comperssor functional data.<P>From GM TSB 89-1-2: "The V5 is a variable displacement compressor that meets air conditioning demand by changing it's stroke instead of cycling a clutch."||||reg||1
Z||000010||Predator||08-14-2000||04:54 PM||||Thanks guys for all the responses. The fog/mist has a odor to it as well. It is not a sticky film, and I have tried different settings on the AC/Heater to get it to stop. Nothing works. I tried taking it to a shop and they didn't want to fool with it. I haven't a clue what is causing it. I have had and have several cars with AC and they don't do this so I think it can be fixed. Something has to cause it. I will probably try and find a mechanic that specializes it AC and have them look at it. I don't think it is humidity because it bellows out of the vents at times. Anyway thanks for trying.<BR>  ||||reg||9
Z||000011||Phil||08-14-2000||05:24 PM||||Just a shot in the dark but are you losing coolant perchance?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||08-15-2000||03:09 PM||||I'd say the heater core let go. may not be enough to easily see coolant drop. You need to get the cover off the thing and have a look for coolant.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Predator||08-15-2000||03:45 PM||||Thanks I will check the Heater Core. It does have a odor to it, and I did have to put some coolent in it. It wasn't a big loss, but it maybe it coul dbe enough in the heater core to cause this and not leak on the floor. It's worth a shot. ||||reg||1
